C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Homework Prompt E. Submit GAMS on Github
      Modify the .gms file for corresponding Transportation problem
       
      THE PROBLEM:
      How many coups and minivans should a car manufacturer produce in a year?
      types of cars:  Coup and Minivan.
       
      Data are as fol-lows:
      Resource Input  Vehicle Type    Resource Availability
         
                           Coup           Minivan            Resource Availability
      Metal                1,000 lbs      2,000 lbs          4,000,000 lbs
      Circuit boards       4 boards       3 boards           12,000 boards
      Labor                5 days         2.5 days           17,500 days
      Profit per vehicle   $6,000         $7,000
       
      Inputs or Profit        Crops        Resource Availability
       
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      Talha Quddoos
      talha.quddoos@usu.edu
      September 21, 2026
  29   
  30  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  31  * Sets for decision variables and constrainsts
  32  SETS veh types of cars /Coup, Minivan/
  33       res resources available /Metal, Circuit, Labor/;
  34   
  35  * 2. DEFINE input data
  36  PARAMETERS
  37     c(veh) Objective function coefficients ($ per vehicle)
  38           /Coup 6000,
  39          Minivan 7000 /
  40   
  41     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  42            /Metal 4000000,
  43             Circuit  12000,
  44             Labor  17500/;
  45   
  46  TABLE A(veh,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  47                   Metal   Circuit   Labor
  48   Coup           1000      4        5
  49   Minivan        2000      3       2.5;
  50   
  51   
  52  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  53  VARIABLES X(veh) vehicles manufactured (Number)
  54            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  55   
  56  * Non-negativity constraints
  57  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  58   
  59  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  60  EQUATIONS
  61     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  62     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints;
  63   
  64  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(veh, c(veh)*X(veh));
  65  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(veh, A(veh,res)*X(veh)) =L= b(res);
  66   
  67   
  68  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  69  MODEL MANUFACTURING /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  70  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  71  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  72   
  73   
  74  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  75  * Solve the Manufacturing model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  76  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  77  SOLVE MANUFACTURING USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  78   
  79   
  80  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


